==Post-match==
As the Arsenal players ran towards Vieira and Lehmann to celebrate, Ferguson and Keane were seen consoling various players and staff members.<ref name="finalfi">Lawrenson, Motson (2005). Event occurs seconds after the match concludes; live coverage between 17:49pm and 17:51pm.</ref> [[Mark Lawrenson]], the BBC's co-commentator for the final, summarised to his counterpart [[John Motson]]: "Well, we must congratulate Arsenal on the way they took the penalties – they were excellent [...] But I have to say over the course of the 120 minutes, Manchester United have been mugged."<ref name="finalfi"/>

Wenger conceded his opponents were the better side, but praised his team's resolve, telling reporters: "It was important to score the first goal and with neither team scoring it remained tight for a long, long period. There were some times in the second half when we were a bit lucky but we defended very well and to keep a clean sheet is good."<ref name=arspost>{{cite news |title=Wenger proud of players after win |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/4562295.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=21 May 2005 |access-date=24 April 2016}}</ref> He admitted his players had practised taking penalties, but was quick to point out "you don't score because of the practising – keeping your nerve is more important."<ref name=arspost/> Lehmann, who had been side-lined by Wenger during the course of the season, credited his teammates for scoring all five penalties, and described it as a "big mental achievement".<ref name=arspost/> Cole called Arsenal's win a "...great team performance, we didn't have too many chances but we defended really well and battled really hard."<ref name=arspost/> Henry, who sat out the final due to injury expressed sympathy for Manchester United, and recollected a similar experience from his early Arsenal career: "I know how they feel because [[2001 FA Cup final|we lost against Liverpool]] and did not deserve to lose. If your name is on the cup you win it."<ref name=post>{{cite news |title=Ferguson hails his Manchester United heroes |url=http://www.independent.ie/sport/soccer/ferguson-hails-his-manchester-united-heroes-26208468.html |newspaper=Sunday Independent |location=Dublin |date=22 May 2005 |access-date=24 April 2016}}</ref>
[[File:Wayne Rooney 2.jpg|thumb|upright|200px|[[Wayne Rooney]] (pictured in 2006) was chosen as man of the match.]]
{{Quote box|width=275px|bgcolor=#FFFFF0 |align=left|quote="The coach decided to change the system to 4–5–1. It was different. We used to win by scoring lots of goals, but this time we won by being strong mentally and defensively. The only way we were going to win this final was on penalties."|salign=left|source=[[Robert Pires]], post match<ref>{{cite news |first=Henry |last=Winter |title=Arsenal make cautious game pay |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2360037/Arsenal-make-cautious-game-pay.html |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=23 May 2005 |access-date=24 April 2016}}</ref>}}

Ferguson was proud of his team's performance, but admitted their failure throughout the season to convert chances into goals, had cost them once more.<ref name=post/> Of the game, he continued: "In cup football, you need a break and we didn't get one. We've had luck in the past, so you understand it can happen. It's not a nice experience but it's one you have to accept."<ref name=post/> Ferguson criticised the referee for failing to send Vieira off during extra-time as he fouled Rooney, and labelled Arsenal as "boring" for deploying negative tactics.<ref name=mupost>{{cite news |title=Ferguson accuses Arsenal's Vieira |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/4561497.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=21 May 2005 |access-date=24 April 2016}}</ref> Keane, like his manager, rued the missed opportunities and said it was a small consolation: "We dominated but I'm sure the Arsenal players won't be too bothered about that – they've got the winners' medals and the cup and we haven't."<ref name=mupost/>

Writing for ''[[The Daily Telegraph]]'', pundit [[Alan Hansen]] felt the ease in which Manchester United dominated the final and Arsenal's inability to vary tactics highlighted why Wenger needed to make changes in the close season.<ref name=hansen>{{cite news |first=Alan |last=Hansen |title=Success can't hide need for major surgery |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/columnists/alanhansen/2360038/Success-cant-hide-need-for-major-surgery.html |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=23 May 2005 |access-date=24 April 2016}}</ref> Hansen agreed with Ferguson that United's lack of goal threat cost them on the day, but felt their future was rosier than Arsenal's.<ref name=hansen/> Nonetheless, he was of the opinion that Chelsea manager [[José Mourinho]] had little to be concerned about, concluding his piece with the sentence: "A London club did come away from Cardiff as big winners but it was not Arsenal, it was Chelsea."<ref name=hansen/> In the same newspaper, [[Paul Hayward (journalist)|Paul Hayward]] praised the performances of Rooney and Ronaldo – "surely the best one-club pairing of under-21s in world football,"<ref>{{cite news |first=Paul |last=Hayward |title=Wonder boys lift United morale |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2360039/Wonder-boys-lift-United-morale.html |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=23 May 2005 |access-date=24 April 2016}}</ref> while ex-Arsenal player [[Alan Smith (footballer, born 1962)|Alan Smith]] noted his former club's win demonstrated how Wenger "for the first time, practically, in his nine-year Highbury tenure, had set up his side with the opposition in mind."<ref>{{cite news |first=Alan |last=Smith |title=Wenger's changing ways |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/columnists/alansmith/2360044/Wengers-changing-ways.html |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=23 May 2005 |access-date=24 April 2016}}</ref> Capturing United's sombre mood, ''[[The Times]]'' football correspondent [[Matt Dickinson (journalist)|Matt Dickinson]] wrote: "The black shirts turned out not to be in protest at Glazer but a reflection of their mood after the first FA Cup Final to be decided by a penalty shoot-out."<ref>{{cite news |first=Matt |last=Dickinson |title=Arsenal punish United's mistakes |pages=S1–2 |newspaper=[[The Times]] |date=23 May 2005}}</ref>

The match was broadcast live in the United Kingdom by both the BBC and [[Sky Sports]], with [[BBC One]] providing the free-to-air coverage and Sky Sports 1 being the pay-TV alternative.<ref>{{cite news |title=Sport on TV: The week ahead |url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/world-news/sport-on-tv-the-week-ahead-1607945 |newspaper=Sunday People |location=London |date=15 May 2005 |access-date=24 April 2016}}</ref> BBC One held the majority of the viewership, with a peak audience of 12.8&nbsp;million (67.1% viewing share), which made it the most-watched final in nine years.<ref name=ratings>{{cite news |title=12.8m see Arsenal lift FA Cup |url=http://www.broadcastnow.co.uk/128m-see-arsenal-lift-fa-cup/1024814.article |journal=Broadcast |publisher=Top Right Group |date=23 May 2005 |access-date=1 September 2015}} {{subscription required}}</ref> The match itself was watched by 10&nbsp;million viewers (61%), and coverage of the final averaged at 7.3&nbsp;million (50.5%).<ref name=ratings/> Viewing figures compiled by ''[[The Guardian]]'' showed the BBC's coverage was second only to ITV's broadcast of the [[2005 UEFA Champions League Final]] between [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] and [[A.C. Milan]], which amassed 13.9&nbsp;million viewers.<ref>{{cite news |first=Owen |last=Gibson |title=ITV tops 2005 sports ratings with Champions League final |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2005/dec/22/broadcasting.ITV |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=22 December 2005 |access-date=24 April 2016}}</ref> Global audience figures for the 2005 FA Cup Final totalled 484&nbsp;million.<ref>{{cite web |title=A history of the FA Cup |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/More/History |publisher=The Football Association |access-date=18 May 2016}}</ref>

==Aftermath and legacy==
[[File:Patrick Vieira NYCFC.JPG|thumb|upright|[[Patrick Vieira]] was sold to Juventus in the summer of 2005.]]
The 2005 final was Vieira's last match as an Arsenal player; he joined [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] in the close season for a combined total fee of €20&nbsp;million.<ref>{{cite news |title=Arsenal accept Juve's Vieira bid |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/a/arsenal/4670829.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=14 July 2005 |access-date=18 May 2016}}</ref> Wenger's decision to sell his captain was made so the team could benefit from Fàbregas, who broke into the first eleven during the season.{{citation needed|date=September 2020}} In later years, Wenger deviated from his usual counter-attacking style, and imposed a fluent system, with less emphasis on physicality.<ref>{{cite news |title=Gary Neville says Arsene Wenger's transfer strategy is either naive or arrogant, criticises Alexis Sanchez and Mesut Ozil |url=http://www.foxsports.com.au/football/gary-neville-says-arsene-wengers-transfer-strategy-is-either-naive-or-arrogant-criticises-alexis-sanchez-and-mesut-ozil/news-story/f559ed84e7496c69813def81830d9012?sv=41f0ff2c37bc353f2e31855d39151807 |work=Fox Sports |date=26 August 2015 |access-date=22 May 2016}}</ref> The immediate seasons after Arsenal relocated to the [[Emirates Stadium]] in 2006 saw Wenger sell several experienced players, and integrate more young talent, as a means of fostering an identity with the club.<ref>{{cite news |title=Arsene Wenger: Success – I make it, I won't buy it |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/arsene-wenger-success--i-make-it-i-wont-buy-it-906388.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/arsene-wenger-success--i-make-it-i-wont-buy-it-906388.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |first=Glenn |last=Moore |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=23 August 2008 |access-date=18 May 2016}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Financing for the stadium however meant Arsenal prioritised its expenditure instead of the squad and trophies.<ref>{{cite news |title=Young guns |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2008/aug/31/arsenal |newspaper=The Observer |location=London |first1=Alex |last1=Fynn |first2=Kevin |last2=Whitcher |date=31 August 2008 |access-date=18 May 2016}}</ref> Though Wenger managed to solidify the club's position in the Premier League's top four and secure the necessary funds to pay back its debtors, the 2005 Cup win represented Arsenal's last silverware for nine years. In [[2014 FA Cup final|2014]], he led Arsenal to a record-equalling 11th FA Cup,<ref>{{cite news |title=Arséne Wenger savours FA Cup win over Hull as Arsenal end drought |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/may/18/arsene-wenger-fa-cup-arsenal-hull-trophy-drought|first=David |last=Hytner |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=18 May 2014 |access-date=18 May 2016}}</ref> and became the record equalling most successful manager in the competition's history [[2015 FA Cup final|a year later]], as his side beat [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]].<ref>{{cite news |first=James |last=Walker-Roberts |title=Arsenal 4–0 Aston Villa: Dominant Gunners seal record FA Cup victory |url=http://www.skysports.com/football/arsenal-vs-a-villa/334699 |publisher=Sky Sports |date=31 May 2015 |access-date=18 May 2016}}</ref>

Like Arsenal, Manchester United endured a period of transition after the final. The Glazer's takeover of the club resulted in disaffected fans setting up [[F.C. United of Manchester]], which, as of 2016, has become the largest supporter-owned football club in the United Kingdom.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.thenonleaguefootballpaper.com/latest-news/conference-north-step-two/15437/fc-united-of-manchester-general-manager-andy-walsh-to-step-down/|title=FC United of Manchester general manager Andy Walsh to step down |last=Coney |first=Steven |date=23 March 2016|newspaper=The Non-League Paper |location=London |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160508041739/http://www.thenonleaguefootballpaper.com/latest-news/conference-north-step-two/15437/fc-united-of-manchester-general-manager-andy-walsh-to-step-down/|archive-date=8 May 2016|url-status=live|access-date=18 May 2016}}</ref> The 2005–06 season began poorly for Manchester United, as they were eliminated in the group stages of the Champions League and Ferguson was booed at after United's home loss to [[Blackburn Rovers F.C.|Blackburn Rovers]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Samuel |last=Luckhurst |title=Dismissing Keane was Sir Alex Ferguson's best decision |url=http://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/manchester-united-dismissing-keane-sir-10458588 |newspaper=Manchester Evening News |date=18 November 2015 |access-date=18 May 2016}}</ref> Journalist [[Henry Winter]] in December 2005 opined that Ferguson needed to resign, writing in his column: "Under Ferguson, United became football's answer to the [[The Magic Circle (organisation)|Magic Circle]]. But the magic now drains away and so, next summer, must Ferguson."<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/columnists/henrywinter/2369465/Ferguson-must-go-in-the-summer.html |title=Ferguson must go in the summer |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |first=Henry |last=Winter |date=9 December 2005 |access-date=18 May 2016}}</ref> He stayed, however, and having already called time on Keane's career at United,<ref>{{cite news |title=Keane in shock exit from Man Utd |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/m/man_utd/4449396.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=18 November 2005 |access-date=18 May 2016}}</ref> he began reinvigorating his squad, by signing defenders [[Nemanja Vidić]] and [[Patrice Evra]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Tim |last=Rich |title=Manchester United will not buy in January, says Sir Alex Ferguson |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/dec/16/manchester-united-sir-alex-ferguson |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=16 December 2011 |access-date=18 May 2016}}</ref> United returned to the Millennium Stadium eight months after the FA Cup loss, and beat [[Wigan Athletic F.C.|Wigan Athletic]] to win the [[2006 Football League Cup Final]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Kevin |last=McCarra |title=Rooney runs amok to wreck Wigan's dream |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/feb/27/match.wiganathletic |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=27 February 2006 |access-date=18 May 2016}}</ref> Ferguson guided his team to their first League title in four years the [[2006–07 FA Premier League|following season]], after stern competition from Chelsea, and won a further 13 competitive honours until his retirement in 2013.<ref>{{cite news |title=The 49 trophies of Sir Alex Ferguson – the most successful managerial career Britain has ever known |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/the-49-trophies-of-sir-alex-ferguson-the-most-successful-managerial-career-britain-has-ever-known-8607136.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/the-49-trophies-of-sir-alex-ferguson-the-most-successful-managerial-career-britain-has-ever-known-8607136.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=8 May 2013 |access-date=18 May 2016}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

[[Kevin McCarra]] regards the final as a turning point in the [[Arsenal F.C.–Manchester United F.C. rivalry|rivalry between the two clubs]]: "...Arsenal and United, who could barely be prised apart in 2005, have since gone their separate ways. The signs of divergence were already apparent that afternoon."<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/blog/2008/nov/08/premierleague-arsenal |title=The May day when Arsenal's and United's paths diverged |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |first=Kevin |last=McCarra |date=8 November 2008 |access-date=18 May 2016}}</ref> The match is considered an example of Wenger setting his team up pragmatically and going against his ideals.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/arsenal/10758805/Arsenal-manager-Arsene-Wenger-recalls-the-day-he-played-defensively-and-won-the-FA-Cup.html |title=Arsenal manager Arsène Wenger recalls the day he played defensively and won the FA Cup |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |first=Matt |last=Law |date=10 April 2014 |access-date=22 May 2016 |quote=At the start, I didn't set up like that and suddenly Manchester United were all over us and I realised that physically we were not able to compete, so I said, 'OK let's defend as long as we can'}}</ref> Having later asserted he would never use the 4–5–1 system again, Wenger adopted the formation for Champions League matches and his approach resulted in Arsenal reaching the [[2006 UEFA Champions League Final]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Football: The madness of King Arsene |first=Paul |last=Smith |url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/Football%3A+The+madness+of+King+Arsene%3B+CHAMPIONS+LEAGUE+FINAL%3A+ARSENAL...-a0145740550 |newspaper=Sunday Mirror |location=London |date=14 May 2006 |access-date=14 August 2012}}</ref>

